        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
During extended stays in microgravity, astronauts' bodies undergo physiological changes that increase the risk of illness or injury. To date, surgery in space has not been performed or attempted. As missions become longer in duration and more complex in nature, such as with future multi-year flights to Mars, there is a rising need to develop the capability to perform emergency surgery during space travel.  Simulation of surgery using computer-based virtual reality (VR) has the potential to improve inflight emergency medical care by allowing crewmembers to learn and practice basic surgical techniques during a long-term mission. This study aimed to evaluate computer-based virtual reality (VR) software as a method to train and assess the skill level of a crewmember for performing surgery during space flight. 
<p>
APPROACH: <br>
A state-of-the-art commercial software surgical simulator called LapSim (Surgical Science, Goteborg, Sweden) was assessed. LapSim software allows gravity to be adjusted to mimic the conditions of surgery in microgravity. This was the first evaluation of a VR microgravity surgical simulator.  Subjects underwent a surgical skill assessment at NASA Johnson Space Center (JSC) using inanimate (full-scale mockups) and computer-based VR simulators. Once baseline skill was determined, the subjects were divided randomly into four training groups to receive training. After training, the subjects were evaluated on board the KC-135 using inanimate and VR simulators. 
<p>
The effectiveness and resource, cost and time requirements of both inanimate and VR surgical skill assessment and training were compared. The LapSim was analyzed as a simulation of both microgravity and surgery, as well as a training tool. If needed, improvements to the LapSim VR system were suggested or implemented. This investigation will ultimately lead to developing an evidence-based training protocol for future crewmembers and for addressing inflight surgical emergencies. 
<p>
RESULTS: <br>
A significant increase in tissue injury and task erosion were seen in clip applying, cutting, and grasping in microgravity and a decrease in task attempts that resulted in a successful completion. However, there was no significant difference in the left and right hand path lengths, and the total number of tasks successfully completed on the ground versus in microgravity, for any of the four laparoscopic exercises.
<p>
This study demonstrates decreased efficiency and increased injury to the simulated tissues in performing laparoscopic skills during microgravity as compared to performing these skills in standard gravitational influence. Additional experiments are indicated to further develop and validate VR microgravity surgical simulation.
<p>
        </div>

